# engineMove(Player player)
# Engine turn with a "thinking mannequin" performance layered on top:
#
#   1. chooseMove runs immediately and the move is stashed locally —
#      legality check (move == 0 ⇒ checkmate/stalemate) happens here so
#      we don't perform the whole charade just to report no-moves.
#   2. Realisation delay, random 10–60 ticks (0.5–3 s), during which the
#      mannequin keeps playing whatever prerecording was running. This
#      is the "didn't notice it's my turn yet" beat.
#   3. stopPrerecording: the playback loop sees prerecordingActive=false
#      next tick and exits, freezing the mannequin in place. Pose snaps
#      back to standing.
#   4. Settle pause, random 3–9 ticks. Mannequin just stands there.
#   5. cruiseToFacing to the midpoint of the move's from-square and
#      to-square centres, facing the board centre (-9133.0, 9670.0)
#      with ±10° yaw jitter and 0-10° pitch. Per-move coords keep the
#      mannequin "near the action" instead of always squatting on the
#      same north-edge tile. cruiseToFacing blocks until the
#      cruise+coast pipeline finishes, so step 6 only fires once the
#      mannequin has physically arrived. Y is implicit: collideY drops
#      the mannequin onto the chess-room floor (y ≈ -61).
#   6. Contemplation pause, ~2 s (35–45 ticks). The mannequin is at
#      the board, looking at the position, before "reaching for the
#      piece" — keeps successive moves from feeling instant. 30% of
#      the time the pause splits into thirds with a sneaking-Pose
#      window in the middle ("leaning in to study the board"), always
#      unsneaking before the move below.
#   6½. Score-swing chat (conditional). If chooseMove's score moved
#      by ≥ 200 cp between turns (and we're not inside mate
#      territory, |delta| < 5000), wait 40 ticks and fire chatTaunt
#      (engine-favorable swing → human likely blundered) or chatCope
#      (engine-unfavorable swing → engine blundered / human played
#      well). Quiet turns skip the delay entirely.
#   7. performEngineMove: turn head to from-square, dwell 1-4 ticks,
#      playSelectSound + highlightMoves, dwell 2-20 ticks, turn head
#      to to-square, dwell 1-4 ticks, then clearHighlights +
#      makeMove + applyMoveVisually + displayBoard + move/capture
#      sound. The two head-turns and three dwell windows are what
#      sells the move as "the engine actually deciding" rather than
#      a board flicker.
#   8. sneakWalkTo back to the exact (midX, midZ) from step 5:
#      cruiseToFacing's coast can settle ~1 b off, and the next
#      prerecording's first /tp would visibly snap that residual.
#      Walking the gap shut crouched (the crouch flips on at a
#      random point in the first half of the walk) and easing yaw
#      and pitch from the contemplation pose to the prerecording's
#      starting pose (yaw=0, pitch=72.64) across the same walk hides
#      both the position snap and the head-orientation snap.
#   9. Resume wandering with a fresh random prerecording.
#
# When the chosen prerecording slot set is empty, step 9 is a no-op and
# the mannequin just stands at the play position until the next
# engineMove call.
#
# Early return: after performEngineMove commits the move, sideToMove
# flips to playerColor. If the human clicks their next move before
# we finish the afterMove linger / sneakWalkTo / startRandom-
# Prerecording tail, sideToMove flips BACK to engineColor — at which
# point steps 8 and 9 are skipped (no point cleaning up if the next
# engineMove is already spawned). The check is only safe AFTER step
# 7 because that's when makeMove flips the side; checking earlier
# would always see engineColor (engine's turn pre-commit).
#
# Fast mode: if this call begins within 2.5 s of the previous call's
# completion (lastEngineMoveEnd[player]):
#   - realise (2) and settle (4) upper bounds drop to ~1/3 (so the
#     "I'm noticing my turn" beat doesn't drag on rapid play).
#   - after-move (step before sneakWalkTo) upper bound is INVERTED:
#     ~3× longer, so the mannequin lingers on the move instead of
#     scampering away the moment the piece slides into place.
# Contemplate (6) is intentionally left at full length — that beat
# is the "reading the position" performance, not slack.

@using rychess
@fast

# Defensive: callers (interactEntity legal-move branch, play.msc) are
# expected to have already cleared humanCanMove before invoking us,
# but clearing again here covers any future direct caller and makes
# the invariant "humanCanMove is false for the entire engine turn"
# locally enforced rather than caller-dependent.
@var humanCanMove[player] = false

@define Long t = system::currentTimeMillis()
@define Int move = chooseMove(player)
@define Long elapsed = system::currentTimeMillis() - t

@if move == 0
    @if inCheck(player)
        # @player &c&lCheckmate.
    @else
        # @player &e&lStalemate.
    @fi
    @return
@fi

# Fast mode: if the last engineMove finished within the past 2.5 s,
# compress the realise/settle/after-move delay UPPER bounds so each
# tick range is ~1/3 of normal (rounded). Contemplate (the "reading
# the position" beat) is left alone — that's the read-the-board
# performance and shouldn't shrink with back-to-back moves. The
# lower bounds are preserved everywhere so the minimum delay still
# floors at "human-plausible".
#   realise: random(10,41) → random(10,20)   (range 31 → 10)
#   settle:  random(3,7)   → random(3,4)     (range 4  → 1)
#   after:   random(5,16)  → random(5,38)    (range 11 → 33, 3× LONGER)
#
# The afterMove inversion is deliberate: when the player is moving
# fast, the engine's move shouldn't immediately scamper back to the
# wandering prerecording. Lingering longer keeps the move from
# feeling like a swat-and-run.
@define Long now = system::currentTimeMillis()
@define Boolean fastMode = (now - lastEngineMoveEnd[player]) < 7500L
@define Double realiseUpper = 31D
@define Double settleUpper = 7D
@define Double afterUpper = 16D
@if fastMode
    # @player DEBUG: Fast mode triggered ({{elapsed}} ms); compressing delays.
    @var realiseUpper = 20D
    @var settleUpper = 4D
    @var afterUpper = 38D
@fi

# math::random on Doubles is exclusive on the upper bound; widen by 1
# and clamp to get inclusive ranges that match the spec's "0.5 to 3s"
# and "3 to 9 ticks".
@define Int realiseDelay = Int(math::random(6D, realiseUpper))

@if realiseDelay < 10
    @var realiseDelay = 10
@fi
@var Harha::delay(realiseDelay)

@var mannequin::stopPrerecording(player)

@define Int settleDelay = Int(math::random(3D, settleUpper))
@if settleDelay > 6
    @var settleDelay = 6
@fi
@if settleDelay < 3
    @var settleDelay = 3
@fi
@var Harha::delay(settleDelay)

# Land on the midpoint of the from-square and to-square centres so the
# move plays out from where the mannequin is standing, not from a fixed
# bench seat. Board geometry: A1 centre is (-9134.30, 9671.30), H8 centre
# is (-9131.71, 9668.66), board centre ~(-9133.00, 9670.00); files run +X
# (a→h), ranks run -Z (1→8), so per-step deltas are linear in (file, rank).
@define Int fromSq = moveFrom(move)
@define Int toSq = moveTo(move)
@define Double avgFile = Double((fromSq % 8) + (toSq % 8)) / 2D
@define Double avgRank = Double((fromSq / 8) + (toSq / 8)) / 2D
@define Double xStep = (-9131.71D - (-9134.30D)) / 7D
@define Double zStep = (9668.66D - 9671.30D) / 7D
@define Double midX = -9134.30D + avgFile * xStep
@define Double midZ = 9671.30D + avgRank * zStep

# Yaw to face board centre. Minecraft yaw = atan2(-dx, dz); MSC only has
# single-arg math::arctan (in degrees), so quadrant-correct manually.
# Same pattern as cruiseTick.msc.
@define Double dx = -9133.0D - midX
@define Double dz = 9670.0D - midZ
@define Double numer = -dx
@define Double lookYaw = 0D
@if dz > 0D
    @var lookYaw = math::arctan(numer / dz)
@elseif dz < 0D
    @if numer >= 0D
        @var lookYaw = math::arctan(numer / dz) + 180D
    @else
        @var lookYaw = math::arctan(numer / dz) - 180D
    @fi
@else
    @if numer > 0D
        @var lookYaw = 90D
    @elseif numer < 0D
        @var lookYaw = -90D
    @fi
@fi

# Per-move jitter so repeated arrivals don't pose identically.
@var lookYaw = lookYaw + math::random(-10D, 10D)
@define Double lookPitch = math::random(60D, 70D)

@var mannequin::cruiseToFacing(player, Float(midX), Float(midZ), Float(lookYaw), Float(lookPitch))

# Contemplation pause once the mannequin has arrived at the board.
# Looks like the engine is "reading the position" before reaching for
# the piece. ~2 s; the small ±5 tick jitter keeps repeated moves from
# feeling metronomic.
#
# 30% of the time the contemplation splits into thirds with a sneak window
# in the middle: stand → sneak → stand. Reads as "leaning in to study the
# position" then sitting back up. The trailing stand portion guarantees a
# clean standing pose before makeMove fires; if we sneaked right up to
# the move boundary the piece would visually be picked up by a crouched
# mannequin, which looks wrong.
@define Int contemplate = Int(math::random(20D, 46D))
@if contemplate > 45
    @var contemplate = 45
@fi
@if contemplate < 35
    @var contemplate = 35
@fi
@define Double sneakRoll = math::random(0D, 1D)
@if sneakRoll < 0.3D
    @define Int third1 = contemplate / 3
    @define Int third2 = contemplate / 3
    @define Int third3 = contemplate - third1 - third2
    @var Harha::delay(third1)
    @bypass /execute in minecraft:theta run data merge entity @e[tag=mannequin_rychess,limit=1] {pose:"crouching"}
    @var Harha::delay(third2)
    @bypass /execute in minecraft:theta run data merge entity @e[tag=mannequin_rychess,limit=1] {pose:"standing"}
    @var Harha::delay(third3)
@else
    @var Harha::delay(contemplate)
@fi

# Score-swing chat trigger. chooseMove updated the cache at the very
# top of this engineMove: prevEngineScore = last turn's bestScore,
# curEngineScore = this turn's. delta > 0 = engine's eval moved in
# its favor (human likely blundered); delta < 0 = engine's eval
# moved against it (engine's last move was worse than expected, or
# the human played a strong reply).
#
# Lands AFTER contemplate (the "reading the position" beat) and
# BEFORE performEngineMove (the visible move) — reads as "the bot
# looked at the board, said something, then played its move". The
# 40-tick lead-in is only paid when a swing actually fires, so quiet
# moves don't add latency to the sequence.
#
# Mate-score gate: searchInner returns ~±100000 near forced mate,
# which would produce huge nonsense deltas at game end. Skip the
# message when |delta| >= 5000 — those are mate-territory swings,
# not blunders.
@define Int swingDelta = curEngineScore[player] - prevEngineScore[player]
@define Int absSwing = swingDelta
@if absSwing < 0
    @var absSwing = 0 - absSwing
@fi
@if absSwing >= 200 && absSwing < 5000
    @if swingDelta >= 200
        @var chatTaunt(player, swingDelta)
    @fi
    @var Harha::delay(Int(math::random(5D, 10D)))
@fi

@define String fromName = squareName(moveFrom(move))
@define String toName = squareName(moveTo(move))

@var performEngineMove(player, move)

# Re-open the click gate: the engine's move is committed, so clicks
# from here onward are the human's next-turn input. The new
# interactEntity (if a click is racing the animation tail) will
# clear humanCanMove again before its makeMove. We do NOT re-set
# humanCanMove at the short-circuit @return points below because
# arriving there means a click has ALREADY beaten us — the new
# interactEntity instance has already cleared humanCanMove and
# spawned a fresh engineMove, and re-setting it true here would
# mistakenly re-open the gate during that fresh engine turn.
@var humanCanMove[player] = true

# Player-already-responded short-circuit. performEngineMove flipped
# sideToMove to playerColor via makeMove; a subsequent human click
# flips it back. So sideToMove != playerColor here means the player
# already played their next move while we were still finishing
# this turn's animation tail — no point lingering or walking back.
# Bail before the afterMove linger and the sneakWalkTo + prerecording
# tail, and let the new engineMove (already spawned by interactEntity)
# take over. lastEngineMoveEnd is still updated so future fast-mode
# checks see this turn as ended.
#
# The guard is only safe AFTER performEngineMove because that's when
# makeMove runs — before that, sideToMove != playerColor reflects
# the engine's pre-commit state, not a player response.
@if sideToMove[player] != playerColor[player]
    @var lastEngineMoveEnd[player] = system::currentTimeMillis()
    # @player DEBUG: Returning 1
    @return
@fi

# Close the residual gap from cruiseToFacing's coast settling (~0-1 b
# off target) so the prerecording's first /tp doesn't snap visibly.
# sneakWalkTo shuffles toward exact (midX, midZ) using one or two
# key-direction headings at a time, eases yaw/pitch from the
# contemplation pose to the prerecording's landing pose (yaw=0,
# pitch=72.64) with most of the swing in the second half, and flips
# the crouch on at a random point in the first half. Uncrouches on
# arrival.
@define Int afterMove = Int(math::random(5D, afterUpper))
@if afterMove > 37
    @var afterMove = 37
@fi
@if afterMove < 5
    @var afterMove = 5
@fi
@var Harha::delay(afterMove)

# Re-check: afterMove can be up to ~1.85 s in fast mode, plenty of
# room for the human to click during it. Skip sneakWalkTo +
# prerecording if so.
@if sideToMove[player] != playerColor[player]
    @var lastEngineMoveEnd[player] = system::currentTimeMillis()
    # @player DEBUG: Returning 2
    @return
@fi

@if fastMode
    @var afterMove = Int(math::random(20D, 40D))
    @var Harha::delay(afterMove)
@fi
@var mannequin::sneakWalkTo(player, Float(-9133.0), Float(9668.75))

# Final safety check before starting the next prerecording: if the
# player responded during the walk, skip starting the prerecording
@if sideToMove[player] != playerColor[player]
    @var lastEngineMoveEnd[player] = system::currentTimeMillis()
    # @player DEBUG: Returning 3
    @return
@fi



@var mannequin::startRandomPrerecording(player)

@var lastEngineMoveEnd[player] = system::currentTimeMillis()
